{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11160 - MONDO_grouped_11786_5324_24332",
  "verbalization": "Phenyltoloxamine, through its indication for Urticaria and Angioedema, is linked to Rupatadine, which in turn is associated with Allergic Rhinitis. This suggests a potential pathway where Phenyltoloxamine may influence the development of allergic rhinitis via the Rupatadine pathway. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Phenyltoloxamine could act as a precursor or mediator in the pathophysiology of allergic rhinitis, possibly through shared mechanisms involving the induction of allergic responses. The consistent association with Rupatadine, a drug known to treat allergic rhinitis, supports the hypothesis that Phenyltoloxamine may have a role in the pathogenesis of this condition. The integration of these paths highlights a possible dependency on Rupatadine as a key intermediate in the relationship between Phenyltoloxamine and allergic rhinitis, though further research is needed to elucidate the exact mechanism.",
